<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project DefaultTargets="Build" ToolsVersion="15.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!-- LIB-REF: the standalone reference host. CONFIGURATION (3) IS A DLL SINCE FORK F4G: this host
       imports Release\standalone\libmh.dll (libmh_std.vcxproj) instead of linking the libmh.lib
       archive into itself, so "libmh + a host, no game binary" is measured against a real dynamic
       library rather than against a statically-folded copy of one.

       WHAT MOVED, AND WHAT DID NOT. The /WHOLEARCHIVE property this project was built around (every
       object in the artifact must resolve: it is how three declared-never-defined symbols were
       found, see main.cpp's banner and docs/dead-ends.md) did not weaken, it MOVED ONE LINK OVER.
       libmh_std.vcxproj is the project that now carries /WHOLEARCHIVE:libmh.lib, and it compiles no
       roster TU of its own, so the DLL and the archive are the same object code by construction.

       MH_SPINE_IN_IMAGE IS GONE FROM THIS PROJECT AND ITS ABSENCE IS LOAD-BEARING (G179), for the
       same reason mh_harness.vcxproj does not define it: the spine now lives in another image, so
       mh::state::live(), owner_table() and owner_count() must NOT be magic statics here. A second
       copy would give the host its own region registry, the DLL's rebases would land in the DLL's
       and the host's hash would walk the abandoned addresses, and nothing would go red.

       MH_LIBMH_BUILD STAYS: it is what crt/crt_select.h reads to pick the vendored CRT over the
       original binary's VAs, and this host's own TUs instantiate roster inlines.
       /LTCG is required because mh_common is built with /GL. -->
  <ItemGroup Label="ProjectConfigurations">
    <ProjectConfiguration Include="Release|Win32">
      <Configuration>Release</Configuration>
      <Platform>Win32</Platform>
    </ProjectConfiguration>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ClCompile Include="dispatch_selftest.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="main.cpp" />
    <!-- F4D-PRE (ruling Q5): the VA marshalling thunks are PER-IMAGE now, not part of the archive.
         addr\mh_calls.gen.cpp left libmh.vcxproj's roster so its 19 `mh::call::detail::s_*` shapes
         stop counting as libmh export contract; the roster still REFERENCES a handful of them
         (save_live's verify-mode trampoline calls, plus two order-enqueue rows LIB-REF-SPLIT
         deliberately did not convert), so /WHOLEARCHIVE would leave those unresolved without this
         row. Compiling it here is the same arrangement mh.vcxproj and mh_nettest.vcxproj already
         had, and the same "both images link the copy they use" precedent F4B set for
         mh_net_proto's session_info.cpp.

         IT IS NOT A HOSTED BUILD. This project does not define MH_LIBMH_BUILD, but the thunks do
         not care: they are naked marshalling shims that take the target VA as an argument and name
         no address themselves. What makes a standalone host safe is that no live path here reaches
         one; scan_libmh_vas.py measures what the ARCHIVE carries, and this row takes bytes OUT of
         that subject rather than adding any. -->
    <ClCompile Include="..\mh\addr\mh_calls.gen.cpp" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Label="Globals">
    <VCProjectVersion>17.0</VCProjectVersion>
    <ProjectGuid>{2D6F8A31-47C5-4B0E-9A12-5E8D3C7F1B64}</ProjectGuid>
    <RootNamespace>libref_host</RootNamespace>
    <WindowsTargetPlatformVersion>10.0</WindowsTargetPlatformVersion>
    <ProjectName>libref_host</ProjectName>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props" />
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)|$(Platform)'=='Release|Win32'" Label="Configuration">
    <ConfigurationType>Application</ConfigurationType>
    <UseDebugLibraries>false</UseDebugLibraries>
    <PlatformToolset>v143</PlatformToolset>
    <CharacterSet>Unicode</CharacterSet>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.props" />
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)|$(Platform)'=='Release|Win32'">
    <!-- THE CONFIG-(3) DEPLOYMENT IS A DIRECTORY, and this is it. Release\standalone\ holds exactly
         two files (libmh.dll and libref_host.exe), which is what "libmh + a host, no game binary"
         looks like on disk. The exe moved here at fork F4G because the DLL cannot: the loader
         resolves a static import beside the importer, and Release\ already has a libmh.dll (the
         HOSTED arm, configuration (2)'s). Two spines with one name, kept apart by the only thing
         that distinguishes two deployments: the folder they are deployed into.
         (An XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, which is why this one has none.) -->
    <OutDir>$(SolutionDir)Release\standalone\</OutDir>
    <IntDir>$(ProjectDir)$(Configuration)\</IntDir>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)|$(Platform)'=='Release|Win32'">
    <ClCompile>
      <PrecompiledHeader>NotUsing</PrecompiledHeader>
      <MultiProcessorCompilation>true</MultiProcessorCompilation>
      <WarningLevel>Level3</WarningLevel>
      <Optimization>MaxSpeed</Optimization>
      <PreprocessorDefinitions>WIN32;NDEBUG;_CONSOLE;_CRT_SECURE_NO_WARNINGS;MH_LIBMH_BUILD;%(PreprocessorDefinitions)</PreprocessorDefinitions>
      <ConformanceMode>true</ConformanceMode>
      <LanguageStandard>stdcpp20</LanguageStandard>
      <AdditionalIncludeDirectories>..\libmh;..\mh;..\mh\include;..\mh_common;..\mh_common\include;..\..\mh_net_proto\include;..\libmh\include</AdditionalIncludeDirectories>
      <IntrinsicFunctions>true</IntrinsicFunctions>
      <RuntimeLibrary>MultiThreaded</RuntimeLibrary>
      <EnableEnhancedInstructionSet>NoExtensions</EnableEnhancedInstructionSet>
      <FloatingPointModel>Precise</FloatingPointModel>
    </ClCompile>
    <Link>
      <SubSystem>Console</SubSystem>
      <GenerateDebugInformation>true</GenerateDebugInformation>
      <GenerateMapFile>true</GenerateMapFile>
      <LinkTimeCodeGeneration>UseLinkTimeCodeGeneration</LinkTimeCodeGeneration>
      <!-- The IMPORT library of Release\standalone\libmh.dll, not the archive. A static import is
           correct here and is the deliberate inverse of libmh_dll's rule (see libmh_std.def's
           banner): a standalone host has no configuration to degrade into, so a missing spine
           failing PROCESS LOAD is the loudest correct answer rather than a hazard. -->
      <AdditionalDependencies>$(SolutionDir)Release\standalone\libmh.lib;$(SolutionDir)Release\mh_common.lib;$(SolutionDir)Release\mh_net_proto.lib;kernel32.lib;user32.lib;ws2_32.lib;advapi32.lib;%(AdditionalDependencies)</AdditionalDependencies>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.targets" />
</Project>
